  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes a circular life cycle for batteries, especially showing how resources can move from creation and production through use, then back into recovery or disposal. Along the top, the main forward pathway begins with photosynthesis and natural resources, then proceeds through material production and battery assembly to battery utilization, illustrated with a car. This suggests battery materials can originate from biomass or other natural feedstocks before being turned into battery components and assembled into working devices.\n\nAfter use, the diagram splits into end-of-life options. One route is “second use,” shown with another device, implying batteries may be repurposed before final processing. From there, materials can enter a recovery stage, where both battery materials and current collectors are reclaimed and fed back upward into production steps such as material production or assembly. This creates a recycling loop.\n\nA separate lower-left branch shows disposal. In that route, batteries or materials may undergo power generation and biodegradation. Arrows from this section point back toward photosynthesis and natural resources, indicating a broader environmental return pathway. Overall, the image emphasizes a closed-loop battery system combining manufacture, reuse, recycling, and eventual disposal.",
